{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Pink Water: Haloarchaea of the Great Salt Lake Causeway",
    "date": "2026-10-10",
    "lesson": "In 1959 a railroad causeway cut Utah's Great Salt Lake into two basins. The north arm, starved of fresh inflow, crept past 27% salinity -- nearly saturated brine -- and turned pink. Halobacteria and other extreme halophiles there pack their cytoplasm with potassium and still need a sunscreen: bacterioruberin carotenoids that double as light-harvesting pigment, which is why the brine itself blushes red-orange. Almost nothing else can touch this water: osmotic stress has bred out the competition, grazers are scarce, and dissolved oxygen is thin because so little will dissolve in brine this dense. This level asks a halophile to make a living anyway -- scavenging what little dead biomass drifts by, defending slow-made territory with EPS rather than outrunning threats it cannot outrun, and surviving on carbohydrate and protein scraps shed by dying algae and brine shrimp cysts, since lipid-rich food is rare here.",
    "citation": "Great Salt Lake North Arm hypersaline microbiology; bacterioruberin pigmentation in Halobacteriaceae",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Great Salt Lake North Arm, Utah - hypersaline halophile bloom (>27% salinity), red-pigmented Halobacteria/Salinibacter community"
  },
